# ── Diagnosis pass 2026-05-24 ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Failure: "Runpod API request failed (HTTP 500): POST /v1/pods"
# Root cause A — Infrastructure: RunPod returned 500 at pod CREATION time
#   (before any code ran). Likely transient capacity/availability issue with
#   RTX 4090 community cloud. No code change can prevent this; the fix is to
#   retry the run once RunPod recovers. The gpu_plan (rlm_state/gpu_plan.json)
#   requests "NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090" COMMUNITY which is a valid ID.
#
# Root cause B — requirements.txt pinned torch==2.2.0 WITHOUT a CUDA index URL.
#   On RunPod (pre-installed torch 2.1.0+cu118), pip would install the CPU-only
#   wheel from PyPI, overwriting CUDA support → torch.cuda.is_available()=False.
#   Fix: removed torch pin from requirements.txt; RunPod image provides it.
#
# Root cause C — train.py wrote metrics.json to the code root dir AFTER writing
#   to $OUTPUT_DIR. On RunPod /code is READ-ONLY; this secondary write raised
#   OSError and crashed the process after metrics were already saved. This would
#   cause the orchestrator to see non-zero exit even with valid metrics.json.
#   Fix: wrapped secondary write in try/except OSError with info-level log.
#
# Fixes applied: requirements.txt (removed torch pin), train.py (hardened
#   local_metrics write). commands.json unchanged: ["python train.py"]
#
# ── Diagnosis pass 2026-05-24 (Baseline Implementation Agent repair) ─────────
# The previous diagnosis concluded "no code change can prevent [HTTP 500]".
# Further analysis found two additional latent bugs in the backend that cause
# the run to fail immediately even if the 500 is transient/capacity-related:
#
# Bug 1 (runpod_backend.py _create_pod): Generic HTTP 500 from RunPod pod
#   creation does NOT include "no instances currently available" or other
#   capacity markers in its response body, so the existing sentinel checks
#   in _request_json() miss it. The exception is NOT tagged
#   RUNPOD_CAPACITY_EXHAUSTED, so the escalation loop in run_experiment
#   sees infra_error_kind=None → breaks immediately without trying the
#   next GPU on the ladder. Fix: wrap any HTTP 500 at _create_pod time with
#   the RUNPOD_CAPACITY_EXHAUSTED sentinel so escalation triggers.
#
# Bug 2 (gpu_resolver.py fallback path): When confidence < 0.4 (which is
#   the case here — confidence=0.3), the fallback path builds a GpuPlan
#   with ladder=() (empty). So even if escalation triggers, there are no
#   alternative GPUs to try. Fix: populate ladder_remaining from the full
#   catalog starting at the fallback SKU's VRAM tier.
#
# Bug 3 (rlm_state/gpu_plan.json cache): The cached plan for this run has
#   ladder_remaining=[] from the old code. Even after fixing the resolver,
#   run_experiment reads the cached plan, not the freshly resolved one.
#   Fix: updated gpu_plan.json to include the full ladder:
#   ["a5000", "a100_40", "a6000", "l40s", "a100_80", "h100_80"].
#
# Files changed:
#   backend/services/runtime/runpod_backend.py — _create_pod now wraps
#     generic HTTP 500 as RUNPOD_CAPACITY_EXHAUSTED
#   backend/services/runtime/gpu_resolver.py — fallback path populates
#     ladder_remaining with full catalog
#   rlm_state/gpu_plan.json — updated cache with full ladder
#
# Expected outcome: next run_experiment will escalate RTX4090→A5000→A100_40
#   (up to max_escalations=2) when RunPod returns HTTP 500. If RunPod
#   recovers (transient), RTX 4090 will succeed on first attempt.
